    "description": "Turkmen is an Oghuz Turkic language spoken in Iran as well as Turkmenistan and Afghanistan. Regional varieties, contact with Persian, and different writing traditions connect Iranian Turkmen to a wider linguistic world. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Turkmen", "Turkic languages", "Oghuz", "Language contact", "Writing systems"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:35:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:35:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Turkmen in Iran: Language, Dialects and Writing Traditions",
      "description": "Discover Turkmen's Oghuz Turkic background, regional varieties, Persian connections, and the writing traditions used in Iran and Turkmenistan."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "linguistic-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Turkmen in Iran's linguistic landscape",
        "content": "Turkmen belongs to the Oghuz group of Turkic languages. Its presence in Iran illustrates an essential distinction: a language spoken in Iran need not belong to the Iranian language family. Turkmen's linguistic ancestry connects it with other Turkic languages, while its history also includes sustained interaction with Persian. [s1]\n\nThe language extends beyond a single national setting. Indiana University's language portal identifies communities in Iran and Afghanistan alongside those in Turkmenistan, and names Yomut among varieties spoken in Iran. For readers encountering Turkmen through travel, literature, or family histories, the country associated with a speaker or text provides useful context, but does not identify every feature of their speech. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sound-and-structure",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How the language is organized",
        "content": "Two features offer a practical introduction to Turkmen grammar. Vowel harmony means that vowels in suffixes adjust to the vowels of the stem. Agglutination means that grammatical information is expressed through suffixes attached in an ordered sequence. These patterns help explain why a Turkmen word can carry information that English distributes across several separate words. [s2]\n\nLong and short vowels are another important part of Turkmen's sound system. Iranica uses this distinction when explaining why neighboring Khorasan Turkic should be treated separately rather than simply grouped under Turkmen. Geographical proximity and related ancestry do not remove meaningful structural differences between languages. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "persian-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Persian connections and literary history",
        "content": "Contact with Persian has contributed both vocabulary and elements used in word formation. Iranica distinguishes direct Persian borrowing from words transmitted through Chaghatay, a historical Turkic literary language. It also separates borrowing from spoken Persian from borrowing through literary usage. These routes matter because similar-looking words can reflect different histories of exchange. [s1]\n\nThe same reference account associates the emergence of an eighteenth-century Turkmen literary idiom with Azadi and his son Makhtumquli. Persian elements were present in that literary development from an early stage. Shared vocabulary therefore records cultural and educational connections; it does not change Turkmen's classification as Turkic. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scripts-and-reading",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "One language, different writing traditions",
        "content": "Writing systems introduce a further distinction between communities. Indiana University's account describes Arabic-script usage in Iran and Afghanistan, alongside the Latin alphabet used in Turkmenistan. It traces the Soviet-era sequence from Arabic to Latin and then Cyrillic writing, followed by the reintroduction of Latin writing in the post-Soviet period. That sequence concerns a particular political and educational history and should not be transferred wholesale to Iran. [s2]\n\nFor a newcomer, the practical lesson is to identify both the regional variety and the script of a resource. Recognizing spoken words does not automatically confer the ability to read them in another alphabet. Turkmen in Iran is consequently best understood through its combination of local speech, cross-border relationships, and distinct literacy traditions. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
