    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Talysh, also called Taleshi or Talyshi, is a Northwestern Iranian language spoken in northwestern Iran and the Republic of Azerbaijan. Its northern, central, and southern varieties form a dialect continuum shaped partly by contact with neighboring languages. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Talysh", "Taleshi", "Iranian languages", "Dialect continuum", "Language contact", "Language transmission"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:35:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:35:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Talysh in Iran: Regional Varieties and Language Contact",
      "description": "Explore Talysh's northern, central, and southern varieties, its multilingual surroundings, and research on grammar and intergenerational transmission."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "language-and-region",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A language extending across a border",
        "content": "Talysh is a Northwestern Iranian language encountered in scholarship under spellings including Taleshi and Talyshi. It is spoken in northwestern Iran and across the border in the Republic of Azerbaijan. Linguists conventionally distinguish northern, central, and southern clusters using differences in vocabulary, sounds, and grammar. These labels describe linguistic patterns rather than three self-contained national languages. [s1][s2]\n\nNorthern Talysh is particularly important for understanding why the international border is not a dialect boundary. Northern varieties occur in Azerbaijan and also in Iran. Anbarani, spoken in Anbaran and adjacent villages in Ardabil province, is an Iranian example. A description of “Iranian Talysh” therefore needs to include more than the central and southern clusters. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "continuum-and-contact",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Variation along a continuum",
        "content": "Daniel Paul's comparative research examined Anbarani, Asalemi, and Masali as representatives of the northern, central, and southern areas. The study describes a dialect continuum: linguistic features change across the region rather than dividing neatly into wholly separate systems. Its evidence came from fieldwork in Iran, including elicited words and sentences and recorded narratives. [s2]\n\nThe surrounding languages help explain some of that variation. Paul's account identifies contact with South Azerbaijani, Tati, Gilaki, and Persian. These neighbors belong to different linguistic backgrounds: South Azerbaijani is Turkic, while the others are Western Iranian. Contact consequently needs to be distinguished from ancestry. A borrowed expression or shared construction can reflect multilingual interaction without making the languages genealogically identical. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "grammar-in-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What grammar reveals",
        "content": "A study of Anbarani explains grammatical relationships through case marking and adpositions—words functioning like prepositions or postpositions. The important point for a general reader is that languages distribute meaning differently: a relationship conveyed through an ending in one language may require a separate word or construction in another. The study's account concerns a named Talysh variety and should not erase differences elsewhere in the continuum. [s1]\n\nResearch on comparison offers another example. Hakob Avchyan's study examines Anbarani alongside Northern Talyshi varieties in Azerbaijan. Its abstract reports that degrees of comparison are expressed syntactically through adpositions and function words, rather than dedicated morphological comparison markers. It also identifies influence from Persian and Azerbaijani. Everyday expressions of relative size or degree thus provide evidence of both grammatical structure and a history of language contact. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "transmission-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Transmission and the limits of a snapshot",
        "content": "Paul's research also reported language shift toward Persian and instances in which Talysh was no longer being passed to the next generation. That finding belongs to research based on fieldwork in 2006 and 2007; it does not supply a current rate of decline across every community. [s2]\n\nThe distinction matters when describing a living language. A regional grammar, a recorded narrative, and a survey of family transmission answer different questions. Together, they show why Talysh deserves attention both as a system of related varieties and as a language whose future depends on patterns of everyday use that require continuing, locally grounded research. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Expression of Armenian case meanings in the Anbarāni dialect of the Talyshi language",
        "publisher": "Yerevan State University, Bulletin of Yerevan University B: Philology",
        "url": "https://journals.ysu.am/bulletin-ysu-philology/en/article/view/vol13_no2_2022_pp097-111",
